Optional seed x86_64 Linux routes, v2.

This route creates its own fresh M and runs lex/read/names/check/lower before
x86 generation. It never invokes gn-run, ly-run, out-build or the project
linker on that M. These are phase-local reservations; the adopted manifest,
MEMORY-MAP and bootstrap closure are unchanged.

Absolute header cells, reserved from xg-build/xr-build through output:
192 = current FN index
193 = sole exported main FN index
194 = positive artifact fuel budget
195 = ELF RW segment file offset
196 = profile (0 standalone scalar ELF, 1 context-v11 runtime)

These cells are not MM-STAT-BASE-relative statistics. No shared frontend,
AArch64 generator, linker or future extension may run concurrently with this
phase or allocate these cells while the optional x86 route owns them.

CODE packs eight little-endian bytes per M word, starting at CODE-BASE+0.
CODE-N counts bytes plus one; byte offset p is word CODE-BASE+floor(p/8),
bits 8*(p mod8)..+7. Capacity is8*CODE-CAP-1 bytes. The packed route never
passes CODE-N or CODE contents to the AArch64 generator/layout/output.
FIX records are [disp32-byte-offset, kind, target, 0]:
kind1 FN-relative call, kind2 LABEL-relative branch, kind3 ELF context LEA.
kind4 literal absolute code-relative offset, kind5 RIP-relative literal LEA.
Every displacement and pool offset is checked before patching.
FN FF-CODE is code-byte-offset+1 (zero = unresolved). LABEL packs the SIR
definition index in high32 and code-byte-offset+1 in low32. The prepass rejects
duplicates; relocation reads only low32. Tail admission follows at most64
LABEL/BR continuations through high32 and stops on any effect/value change.
All locations are private to this phase, not AArch64 word indices/fixups.

Guest parameters: rdi,rsi,rdx,rcx,r8. Private context: r9.
Runtime profile parameters6..16: [caller rsp +8*(i-5)] (zero-based i).
The callee reads them at [rbp+16+8*(i-5)]. All call sites align rsp to16.
rbp-8 saves context; local slot s is rbp-8(s+1); temp t is
rbp-8(NSLOTS+2+t). Frames are16-aligned and at most4096 bytes.
The runtime frame adds112 bytes: two scratch words after the declared temps
and a96-byte outgoing argument area. No callee-saved register except rbp is
modified. Single result rax; state results rax/rdx. RES2 preserves rdx across
the preceding call's result move and context reload.
Direct/indirect CALL whose continuation is only LABEL/BR then RET of the same
temp uses leave/jmp when there are at most5 arguments. Stack arguments retain the regular call
path. Source fuel charge sites and callee entry charges are unchanged.

C helpers use the ordinary SysV ABI: rdi=context; rsi,rdx,rcx,r8,r9=operands;
call [rdi+RT-slot]. Context is reloaded from rbp-8 after every helper call and
before every guest call. Pointers/handle validation and allocation budgets
are the existing trusted loader ABI v11's responsibility. No C source
translates Kotoba or emits guest machine instructions.

The ELF profile still rejects handles/runtime operations. The runtime profile
uses an explicitly tagged container, never the architecture-ambiguous KSEED1:
  KX64V1 x86_64-linux sysv-ctx-v11 <payload-length> 0\n\n<payload>
The payload begins with a rel32 jump to main()->i64, then function code and
8-aligned literal data. Strings are pair(code-relative-offset,byte-length),
vectors are v11 handles. The raw payload runs under the trusted x86_64 loader
with offset0/arity0; the Python harness only verifies and copies bytes.
The runtime fuel budget comes from that loader's context, not the container.
The optional Linux bootstrap launcher includes the pinned C loader unchanged
and pre-touches512 KiB of its existing stack before sandbox installation.
The unchanged loader lowers RLIMIT_AS after mapping its fixed arenas, so
post-sandbox heap/stack growth would otherwise fail. glibc reserves16 MiB
of ordinary allocator headroom at startup; the stack reserve stays below
the existing1 MiB stack budget. No syscall grant or process limit is widened.

X86_64-LINUX-MANIFEST retains all adopted AArch64/project routes in the
optional AArch64 cross compiler. X86_64-LINUX-SELFHOST-MANIFEST builds a
source-only Linux compiler with the same reader/checker/lowerer and x86
backend. Its driver requires the explicit x86 target and format; it refuses
project/KIR/source-path/policy routes rather than discarding their flags.
Both profiles reject floating-point signatures/operations before output.
State templates have no standalone SIR and are skipped exactly as lw-fns
requires; all concrete specializations are admitted. Generated closure
adapters have no parameter type table; their checked lambda is admitted.
No externally callable scalar ABI, signed artifact admission or Q9 claim.
